<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌧️ LIVE PRECIPITATION</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 9:27PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> 9:27PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE PRECIPITATION</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT OBSERVATION (NEXRAD 5-min mosaic)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/precipitation/legend.png?v=iem-n0q-20261005-0125" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Precipitation intensity (NEXRAD N0Q base reflectivity, dBZ, OBSERVED radar echo): <b>LOWEST 5 Very Light</b> cyan/blue (drizzle, mist) &rarr; <b>Light</b> greens (~20-30: light rain/snow) &rarr; <b>Moderate</b> dark green (~30-40: steady rain/snow) &rarr; <b>Heavy</b> yellow/orange (~40-50: heavy rain) &rarr; <b>Severe</b> red (~50-60: severe storms, hail possible) &rarr; <b>Intense</b> magenta/purple (~60-70: violent storms, large hail) &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 70+ Extreme</b> white (destructive hail, debris). Same dBZ always shows the same color in every season — rain, snow, lake-effect snow, thunderstorms. No echo (no precipitation) stays see-through so the map underneath shows.</p><p><b>Source:</b> Iowa Environmental Mesonet (Iowa State University) CONUS NEXRAD N0Q Base Reflectivity mosaic from NOAA NWS WSR-88D Level-III (5-minute updates)<br/><a href="https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/current/radar.phtml">https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/current/radar.phtml</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE PRECIPITATION</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 9:27PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> 9:27PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE PRECIPITATION</h2><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/precipitation/legend.png?v=iem-n0q-20261005-0125" width="600" alt="precipitation gradient key"></p><p>Precipitation intensity (NEXRAD N0Q base reflectivity, dBZ, OBSERVED radar echo): <b>LOWEST 5 Very Light</b> cyan/blue (drizzle, mist) &rarr; <b>Light</b> greens (~20-30: light rain/snow) &rarr; <b>Moderate</b> dark green (~30-40: steady rain/snow) &rarr; <b>Heavy</b> yellow/orange (~40-50: heavy rain) &rarr; <b>Severe</b> red (~50-60: severe storms, hail possible) &rarr; <b>Intense</b> magenta/purple (~60-70: violent storms, large hail) &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 70+ Extreme</b> white (destructive hail, debris). Same dBZ always shows the same color in every season — rain, snow, lake-effect snow, thunderstorms. No echo (no precipitation) stays see-through so the map underneath shows.</p><p>How to read it in any season. <b>Light</b> greens are light rain in summer — and light snow in winter. <b>Moderate</b> dark green is steady rain or steady snow, including lake-effect bands. <b>Heavy</b> yellow/orange is heavy rain (or very heavy snow rates). <b>Severe</b> red means severe thunderstorms with hail possible; <b>Intense</b> magenta/purple means violent storms with large hail and flash flooding; <b>Extreme</b> white is reserved for the most destructive echoes. Radar sees precipitation particles, not air temperature, so one scale covers rain, snow, and thunderstorms all year. Where you see through the overlay, there is no precipitation right now.</p><p><b>Variable:</b> N0Q base reflectivity (dBZ): raw radar echo strength from the NOAA WSR-88D network, composited every 5 minutes. Shows rain, snow, and thunderstorms in all seasons; it does not classify type, it shows intensity.<br/><b>Units:</b> dBZ<br/><b>Source:</b> Iowa Environmental Mesonet (Iowa State University) CONUS NEXRAD N0Q Base Reflectivity mosaic from NOAA NWS WSR-88D Level-III (5-minute updates)<br/><b>Update:</b> mosaic rebuilt every 5 minutes; Google Earth re-polls every 60 seconds<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/current/radar.phtml">IEM / NOAA NEXRAD</a></p>]]></description>
